{
  "stage": "write",
  "item": "ch04.s08",
  "inputs_hash": "54a14b11a30f50c2a4d466ae10cd1a555b47dc62025087616b47d4991e30ca70",
  "output_hash": "4c4e5b47bb02bde3b5621e201b507809b1c62b51b474b248759fe167cc59c1bd",
  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch04.s08",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch04.s08.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p01.01",
            "text": "How can next-token predictions be turned into a number that tells whether a language model is doing well, and why does that number matter for training?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p01.02",
            "text": "In traditional machine learning, regression generally uses least-square or mean-squared-error losses, while classification uses losses such as cross-entropy or hinge loss.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@20",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@23",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@27",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@29",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@32",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@34",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@36",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@39"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p01.03",
            "text": "A loss function is a quantitative metric that captures how good or bad the large language model's output is.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@187",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@190",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@195",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@198",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@200",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@204",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@206",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@209"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p01.04",
            "text": "If a loss function quantifies LLM performance, then training can aim to minimize that loss so the model does better and better.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@204",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@206",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@209",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@212",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@215"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p01.05",
            "text": "Once a loss function is constructed, gradient-descent-based backpropagation algorithms can be used to train the model.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@218",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@220",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@223",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@225"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p01.06",
            "text": "Here, text evaluation means defining a loss function that compares what the model generated with the good output that is actually wanted.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@258",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@260",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@262",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@264",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@267",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@270",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@273"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p01.07",
            "text": "That loss is determined by the predicted values, the target values, and how close they are.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@503",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@506",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@509",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@511",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@513",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@518",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@519",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@521"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p01.08",
            "text": "Because a randomly initialized LLM does not initially produce the outputs that are wanted, it must be trained.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@715",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@717",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@720",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@722"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch04.s08.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p02.01",
            "text": "The loss is built from the probabilities assigned to the correct next-token indices in the model output.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1832",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1835",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1837",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1841",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2084",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2087",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2089",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2091"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p02.02",
            "text": "Before training, those correct-index probabilities are not necessarily the highest, because the model is not trained yet.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1888",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1890",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1892",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1894"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p02.03",
            "text": "To evaluate the model, the probability tensor is indexed at the target positions for each token prediction in each batch, and those probabilities are collected together.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1897",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1899",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1901",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1903",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1905",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1907",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1910",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1912",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2743",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2745",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2747"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p02.04",
            "text": "With two batches and three token predictions in each batch, there are six target probabilities in total: \"p11\", \"p12\", and \"p13\" from the first batch, and \"p21\", \"p22\", and \"p23\" from the second.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1982",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1984",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1987",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1991",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1992",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1994",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1997",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@1999",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2037",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2039",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2041",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2043",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2045",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2048",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2049"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p02.05",
            "text": "The training goal is to make all six selected target probabilities as close to one as possible, because then the highest-probability output indices will line up with the target indices.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2002",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2004",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2007",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2009",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2012",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2014",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2016",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2018",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2021",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2026",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2027",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2030",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2033",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2035"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p02.06",
            "text": "Conceptually, the workflow starts from the logits tensor, converts it to probabilities with softmax, identifies the target-index probabilities, merges them together, and computes the loss from those values.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2075",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2076",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2079",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2081",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2084",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2087",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2098",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2101",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2103",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2107"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch04.s08.p03",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p03.01",
            "text": "Because the problem is expressed in probabilities, logarithms and cross-entropy are the natural tools to use.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2113",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2115",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2118",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2120",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2122",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2124"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p03.02",
            "text": "Rather than working directly with the target probabilities, it is better mathematically and from an optimization perspective to take their logarithms, average them, and then negate the result.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2124",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2126",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2127",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2129",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2131",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2133",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2136",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2138",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2140",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2142"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p03.03",
            "text": "The negative log-likelihood is the negative of the mean of the log target probabilities.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2160",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2162",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2167",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2168",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2171",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2174",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2190",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2193",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2198"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p03.04",
            "text": "For these six collected probabilities, the negative log-likelihood is the negative of the sum of log \"p11\" through log \"p23\" divided by 6.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2174",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2178",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2181",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2185",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2198",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2201",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2203",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2207"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p03.05",
            "text": "Using the negative form makes the objective intuitive, because the task becomes minimizing a loss instead of maximizing it.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2232",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2234",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2237",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2239",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2241",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2244",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2247",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2250",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2251",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2253",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2256",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2260",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2270",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2272"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p03.06",
            "text": "This negative log-likelihood is also called cross-entropy loss.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2221",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2223",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2225",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2324",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2328",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2331",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2333"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p03.07",
            "text": "Cross-entropy loss essentially measures the difference between two probability distributions, and in this setting it can be viewed as categorical cross-entropy.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2364",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2367",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2369",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2370",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2372",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2374",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2376",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2378",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2380"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p03.08",
            "text": "Minimizing cross-entropy pushes the target probabilities toward one, so the loss should be as low and as close to zero as possible.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2211",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2216",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2218",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2221",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2223",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2225",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2227"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p03.09",
            "text": "If the loss reaches zero, that indicates the mean is effectively at one and the target probabilities are at or near one, which is the training goal.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2276",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2278",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2281",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2282",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2285",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2287",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2290",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2293",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2295"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p03.10",
            "text": "When this loss is minimized, the predicted next-token indices align closely with the target indices, which indicates that the language model is performing well.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2333",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2337",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2338",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2340",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2343",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2346",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2348",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2350",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2351",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2354",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2356",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2358"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch04.s08.p04",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p04.01",
            "text": "The same computation can be written explicitly in code before using a library helper.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p04.02",
            "text": "A first coding step is to explicitly find the target probabilities \"p11\", \"p12\", \"p13\", \"p21\", \"p22\", and \"p23\" from the model output probabilities.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2696",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2698",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2701",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2706",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2710",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2712",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2715"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p04.03",
            "text": "For the first batch, the code looks at rows 0, 1, and 2 and picks the probability values at target indices 3626, 610, and 345.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2851",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2854",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2856",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2858",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2862",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2864",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2868",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2872",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2874",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2876",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2879",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2880",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2883",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2885"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p04.04",
            "text": "For the second batch, it again looks at rows 0, 1, and 2 and picks the probability values at target indices 1107, 588, and 11311.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2888",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2890",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2892",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2894",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2897",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2899",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2902",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2904",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2907",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2908",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2911",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2913",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2916"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p04.05",
            "text": "After selecting them, the six probabilities are concatenated, their logarithms are taken, the mean of those log values is computed, and then the negative is taken to obtain the negative log-likelihood loss.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2932",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2934",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2937",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2941",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2944",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2945",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2947",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2949",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2951",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2953",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2955",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2957",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2960",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2962",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2964"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p04.06",
            "text": "In deep learning, it is conventional to use negative log-likelihood rather than positive log-likelihood so that the loss is minimized instead of maximized.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2967",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2970",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2972",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2975",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2976",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2978",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2980"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch04.s08.p05",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p05.01",
            "text": "In practice, the loss is usually computed in a simpler way.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p05.02",
            "text": "Before computing the loss with the helper function, the two batches are flattened so the output tokens become one sequence and the target indices become one sequence as well.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2382",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2384",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2386",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2393",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2405",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2407",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2410",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2414",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2421",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2451",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2453"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p05.03",
            "text": "The logits are flattened from shape 2 by 3 by 50257 to shape 6 by 50257 by merging the batch and token dimensions.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2386",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2389",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2391",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2393",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3002",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3003",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3008",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3010",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3012",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3014"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p05.04",
            "text": "The target tensor is flattened from two rows and three columns into six values.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2424",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2427",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2451",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2453",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3025",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3027",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3029",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3031",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3033",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3035"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p05.05",
            "text": "After flattening, the target sequence is 3626, 610, 345, 1107, 588, and 11311, and these indices identify the entries whose probabilities correspond to \"p11\" through \"p23\".",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2453",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2455",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2460",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2464",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2466",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2468",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2471",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2473",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2475",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2477",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2480",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2484",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2486",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2488",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2489",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2492"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p05.06",
            "text": "The call \"torch.nn.functional.cross_entropy(logits_flat, targets_flat)\" computes the loss from flattened logits and flattened targets.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2502",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2504",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2508",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2511",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2514",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2515",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3256",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3258",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3261",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3263"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p05.07",
            "text": "This function first applies softmax to the logits to convert them into probabilities, then finds the probabilities at the target indices, takes the negative log, and averages them.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2520",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2522",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2525",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2527",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2529",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2532",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2534",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2537",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2539",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2541",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2544",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2547",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2551",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2553",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2556",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2558",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3040",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3042",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3045",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3047",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3049",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3051"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p05.08",
            "text": "A single call performs all the required steps and returns the final loss between the output and the target.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2558",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2560",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2565",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2567",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2571",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2573",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2576",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2577",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2580",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2581"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p05.09",
            "text": "Conceptually, inputs such as \"every effort moves\" and \"I really like\" pass through the GPT architecture to produce logits, the batches are merged into \"logits_flat\", the target-index probabilities are examined, and the cross-entropy loss is computed from them.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2599",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2601",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2604",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2605",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2608",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2610",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2612",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2614",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2617",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2619",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2621",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2624",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2626",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2652",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2654",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2656"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p05.10",
            "text": "In an ideal model, the probabilities at the target indices would be close to one, but in an unoptimized LLM they are typically small, so training aims to increase them by minimizing cross-entropy.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2629",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2631",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2632",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2634",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2638",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2641",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2643",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2645",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2647",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2649",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2652",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2654",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2656"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p05.11",
            "text": "The function is described as finding the cross-entropy loss between the input logits and the target.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2672",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2674",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2677",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2681",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@2683"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p05.12",
            "text": "The goal during training is to reduce this categorical cross-entropy loss as close to zero as possible.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3053",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3055",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3057",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3059",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3062",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3063",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3065",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3067"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch04.s08.p06",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p06.01",
            "text": "Cross-entropy is not the only way to report model quality.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p06.02",
            "text": "Perplexity is another measure of loss, described as a minor modification of cross-entropy that measures how well the model's predicted probability distribution matches the actual distribution of words in the dataset.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3069",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3071",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3075",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3077",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3080",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3082",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3085",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3087",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3089"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p06.03",
            "text": "It is presented as a more interpretable way to understand model uncertainty in predicting the next token.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3089",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3092",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3094",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3096"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p06.04",
            "text": "A lower perplexity score means better predictions.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3101",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3103"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p06.05",
            "text": "Perplexity is calculated as the exponent of the loss.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3105",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3107",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3110",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3272",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3275",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3277"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p06.06",
            "text": "In the example, the loss is 10.79 and the perplexity is 48725.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3283",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3285",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3288"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p06.07",
            "text": "A perplexity of 48725 means the model is roughly as uncertain as if it had to choose the next token randomly from about 48725 tokens in the vocabulary.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3116",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3119",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3121",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3124",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3126",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3130"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p06.08",
            "text": "For the input \"every effort moves\", that means the current model is at a stage where predicting the next token is like choosing among 48725 possible tokens, which indicates high uncertainty and poor performance.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3139",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3142",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3146",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3149",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3150",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3155",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3158",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3160",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3162"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p06.09",
            "text": "If the perplexity were 2, the model would effectively need to choose between only two tokens, which would indicate a very accurate model.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3162",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3165",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3166",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3168",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3171"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p06.10",
            "text": "A perplexity of 48725 also suggests that about 48000 tokens are effectively equally likely to be the next token, so the model is not good at all.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3171",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3174",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3176",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3180",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3182",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3185"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch04.s08.p06.11",
            "text": "Perplexity is usually more interpretable than a raw categorical cross-entropy value such as 10, because it can be related directly to the vocabulary size.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3187",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3189",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3191",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3193",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3195",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3197",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3201",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3203",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3205",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3288",
              "7TKCrt--bWI@3290"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
